{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 23, :comments ["<b>if they shall see, etc. and all who provoked Me, etc.</b> One needs to know why it was necessary to specify the provokers — from where would it occur to the mind that they would not be included in the decree, such that it was necessary to particularize them? And it is possible that since HaShem said ״וַיְנַסּוּ אוֹתִי זֶה עֶשֶׂר פְּעָמִים״, which indicates that whoever did not test Him ten times — and they are those who left Egypt under the age of twenty — are not included in the decree, therefore He excluded the provokers from the general rule: for even if among them were some who had not tested Him ten times, they were sealed under this decree. And His provokers are the spies, or those who were casting stones at the cloud of HaShem, according to the words of our Rabbis of blessed memory (Sotah 35a)." "<b>Also,</b> the verse further intends to record a new particular regarding the spies: that they would not be worthy of the land — meaning, they would not take a portion in it. And as they said in tractate Bava Batra (117a) that the sons of the spies did not take the spies' portion in the land, but rather the portion of their father's father, end quote — unlike the rest of that entire generation, whose sons did take their portion. And the meaning of ״יִרְאוּהָ״ is that they would not be worthy of it. Therefore He concluded by saying <b>but My servant Caleb</b>, etc., <b>and his seed shall possess it</b>, and knowledge of the contrasts is grasped from what is said."], :book :numbers, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 14, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T20:09:17.927731651Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}